Bob Harris wrote:
> In article <RZCdnRYwl51w2oPcRVn-gQ@magma.ca>,
>  Mario Stargard <mstargard@myprivacy.ca> wrote:
> 
> 
>>Uwe M�ller wrote:
>>
>>>Dear all,
>>>what is the easiest was to clone an ES40 local system disk, including 
>>>several partitions and swap, to another empty disk of equal size, which 
>>>is also located within the ES40 drive bay.
>>>I am using TRU64 V5.1a
>>>
>>>Best regards,
>>>Uwe
>>
>>If you have the advfs utilities, I'd use the addvol utility to add 
>>partitions to the advfs volumes; then remove the originals.  The nice 
>>thing about this approach is that you don't need to reboot.  Don't 
>>forget to set the new boot device in the srm with consvar.
>>
>>Otherwise, the old dump and restore from one disk to the other works 
>>fine.  Something like this should work:
>>
>>mkfdmn /dev/dsk1a newroot_domain
>>mkfdmn /dev/dsk2a newusr_domain
>>mkfset newroot_domain root
>>mkfset newusr_domain usr
>>mkfset newusr_domain var
>>mount newroot_domain#root /mnt
>>cd /mnt
>>vdump 0f - / | vrestore xf -
>>mount newusr_domain#usr /mnt/usr
>>cd usr
>>vdump 0f - /usr | vrestore xf -
>>and so on for /var
>>
>>Then go to /mnt/etc/fdmns and change the symlinks in the domain 
>>directories to point to the correct place.
>>
>>Don't forget to reassign swap in /etc/sysconfigtab.  If you use the 
>>advfs utilities you'll need to reassign swap and reboot to get it to 
>>take effect because you cannot remove swap space once it is assigned.
>>
>>Mario
> 
> 
> This does NOT apply to the boot volume.  AdvFS only allows 1 volume in 
> the boot (root) volume.  However, /usr, /var, and another other file 
> system/domains can use the addvol/rmvol trick.
> 
> The reason the boot root domain can not have more than one disk, is that 
> the boot firmware does not know how to deal with multiple disks in order 
> to find all the parts of vmunix.  And it can be a chicken and an egg 
> situation since /etc/advfs/... is where the list of disks are stored, 
> and if the for some reason /etc/advfs is _NOT_ on the disk specified to 
> be booted, there is no way to find the other disks.
> 
>                                         Bob Harris
Doh.  You're right.

Mario



