Side note.  Most of these 32 bit overflow issues were not seen, simply 
because at the time, you were lucky if a disk held 1GB, which means a 
1.5TB file system would need about 1,500 disks, which would be 20'ish 
racks of disks.  Not many people were throwing that many disks together 
into one file system.

But then we started getting 2GB disks, then 4GB, then 9GB, then 18GB, 
then 36GB, 60GB, 120GB, 160GB, 250GB, etc... and now it only takes maybe 
6 250GB disks to give us 1.5TB.  Heck, you can by PC/Mac systems with 
3/4 to a full terabyte of storage inside the tower or even a 1U rack 
mount.

And this kind of storage started to show up during the development of 
v5.0, so a lot of 32 bit overflow issues were handled during that 
development cycle, along with expanded statfs(), statvfs(), stat() 
system calls that were implemented so that these huge sizes could be 
delivered to df, ls, du, and any other utility that wants to know how 
big things are.

Changing kernel system call APIs is not the kind of thing you can do in 
a patch, so v4.* has the original statfs(), statvfs(), and stat() system 
call APIs.

If you would care to upgrade to v5.1b, I'm sure a lot of these issues 
would go away, although I can not speak for Samba, or non-Tru64 UNIX 
nfsv3 clients.  Heck, I can't even speak for myself :-)

                                        Bob Harris


