Rob wrote:

> Hi folks,
> 
> I am a newb UNIX admin and my Compaq P166 is crashing to the bios
> prompt once every 2-3 months.  I have no idea why, and its driving me
> nuts.  I suspect it is a RAM issue, but whenever it crashes, there is
> no user activity on the server.  I know this because it is our backup
> machine.  Can anyone give me any clues?  Thanks a million!!

>   biu_stat    = 0x1440

Indicates "data parity error during dcache fill operation".

>   fill_adr    = 0x2319580

Memory address being reference at error time.

>   fill_syndrome       = 0x80

Indicates high longword in error.

>   bc_tag      = 0x400a94

Bit 0 = 0, indicates Bcache miss, hence parity error from memory.

>   coma_base0  = 0x0
>   coma_base1  = 0x200
>   coma_base2  = 0x47ff0000
>   coma_cnfg0  = 0x47ff0007
>   coma_cnfg1  = 0x47ff0007
>   coma_cnfg2  = 0x47ff0000

You appear to have 4 memory SIMMS, each 64MB in size.  They are arranged as 
two 128MB banks (0 and 1) of memory, with one of each pair providing the 
low longword, the other the high longword.  For this error entry, the SIMM 
reporting the error is the high longword of bank 1, which is in SIMM slot 
J25, which is the 3rd slot from the cpu module.

However, you can't necessarily blame that SIMM for your history of errors.  
You really need to review your error log data or other crashes for similar 
data.  If they all point to the SIMM, then it's likely to be bad.  But, it 
could be the slot, as well.  Try swapping SIMMs between slots, and see if 
the problem moves to a different slot.

If you find that the memory errors are occurring in the high longwords of 
both banks, you may have a problem with the motherboard or cpu module.

Good luck!  If you fix it, you can send the million to me: small unmarked 
bills.  Email me for my shipping address :^)

